<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izates II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izates II was king of Adiabene in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 1st century CE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, he and his mother Queen Helena converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. He helped the Parthian king Artabanus II regain his throne and was rewarded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, and he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the tomb built by his mother.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izates-ii.jpg" alt="Izates II" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Adiabene,_ca_37_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
